markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Unit 1 Curriculum Overview

Unit 1: Logic-Based AI introduces students to what intelligence means, how to write and reason through basic Python, and how rule-based approaches to AI work, and where they break down.

They learn to translate pseudocode into working code, use optimization and simulation to build intuition for how AI systems search for solutions, and build a decision tree they implement themselves in the unit's capstone project.

<div style="background: #FFFFFF; border: 1px solid #E4E2F8; padding: 20px; border-radius: 10px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); font-size: 14pt; line-height: 1.6; max-width: 800px; margin: 20px auto;">
  <h2>Students will:</h2>
  <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; font-size: 14pt">
    <li>Distinguish between different approaches to AI, from rule-based logic to learned behavior, and explain why "intelligence" isn't one single thing.</li><br>
    <li>Write and trace basic Python: variables, functions, and pseudocode-to-code translation.</li><br>
    <li>Use hill climbing and simulation to reason about optimization as a concept, not just a coding pattern.</li><br>
    <li>Debug intelligent systems by reading error messages and tracing logic, not guessing at fixes.</li><br>
    <li>Build a decision tree and implement it in Python, then reflect on where rule-based AI reaches its limits.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

---

